export type MythArticle = {
  mythId: string;
  title: string;
  excerpt: string;
  /** Plain paragraphs. A line starting with "## " becomes a subheading. */
  body: string[];
};

export const MYTH_ARTICLES: Record<string, MythArticle> = {
  titanomachy: {
    mythId: "titanomachy",
    title: "The War That Started in a Nursery",
    excerpt: "Everyone remembers the thunderbolts. The real turning point of the Titan war is a swaddled rock, a goat and a room full of dancers making noise.",
    body: [
      `If I asked you to name the hero of the Titan war, you would probably say Zeus. I'd argue that the real hero is a nursery. Before there is any fighting at all, the war has already been won or lost in a cave on Crete, by a mother, a goat, some nymphs and a group of armed men who are paid to be loud. The thunderbolts come later and get all the credit. I want to spend this piece on the part that gets skipped.`,
      `## A mother's trick`,
      `Rhea had watched her husband Cronus swallow five children. When she was pregnant with the sixth, she went to her parents, Gaia and Uranus, and asked what to do. In Hesiod's account they sent her to Lyctus in Crete, where she gave birth in secret and hid the baby in a cave on Mount Aigaion. Then she wrapped a large stone in baby clothes and handed it to Cronus, and he swallowed it without looking.`,
      `It's easy to laugh at that, and the Greeks did. But think about the situation. The most powerful being in the cosmos, a god who had overthrown his own father with a sickle, was beaten by a piece of laundry. He had learned to fear a son. He had not learned to check what he was eating. The first move of the war is a deception so simple that it only works because it is domestic. A husband doesn't think to inspect what his wife hands him.`,
      `## The noise`,
      `Later poets filled in the nursery. Callimachus, writing in the third century BC, says that the baby was fed on the milk of the goat Amalthea, and on honey brought by bees, and tended by nymphs. And outside the cave stood the Curetes, armed attendants who clashed spears against shields and danced, so that Cronus would never hear the baby crying.`,
      `I love this detail because it is practical. You can't hide a baby's voice. But you can bury it under louder noise. The survival of the king of the gods rests on a group of men making a racket on purpose. It's also an unusually human picture of what raising a child under threat looks like: you are not stronger than the danger, so you make sure the danger can't hear.`,
      `Notice who is in this story. Rhea, Gaia, Amalthea, the nymphs. Almost all of the people who protect Zeus are women, or are in the care of women. The father is the threat and the mothers are the shelter. That pattern shows up again and again in the myths, and I think it says something about how the Greeks pictured power: it was dangerous when it was held tightly, and it survived when it was passed on quietly.`,
      `## The stone that stayed`,
      `The stone itself has a long afterlife. When Zeus was grown and Cronus was forced to bring up what he had swallowed, the stone came out first, because it had gone down last. Hesiod says Zeus set it up at Pytho, under the slopes of Parnassus, as a sign and a wonder for mortals. That is Delphi, and the Greeks believed they could still see it. Pausanias saw a stone there in the second century AD, which the priests anointed with oil daily.`,
      `So the thing that fooled a king turned into a holy object. I like the symmetry. The deception that started the war ends up as the centre of a sanctuary, and the oracle that spoke for Apollo stands on the spot where a rock was once mistaken for a child.`,
      `## Two mountains in one province`,
      `The fighting itself comes across in Hesiod as a sound more than a sequence of events. The Titans held Mount Othrys, the new gods held Olympus, and both mountains are in Thessaly, within sight of each other. The sea roared, the earth groaned, the sky shook, and Olympus trembled from its roots. The tremor, Hesiod says, reached all the way down to Tartarus. For ten years neither side could break the other.`,
      `What I notice is how little of the war is about individual duels. There are no famous fights between named champions, no Hector and Achilles. The whole thing is a collision of forces, which is exactly what you'd expect from a conflict between elemental beings. It reads more like a geological event than a battle. When the Hundred-Handers finally start throwing rocks, three hundred at a time, it's closer to a landslide than a siege.`,
      `## The losers' side`,
      `Our main source is a victor's poem, and it's worth remembering that there was another one. A lost epic called the Titanomachia, attributed to Eumelus of Corinth or to Arctinus, told the war in more detail, and the few surviving fragments suggest it was far less one-sided. Ancient commentators say that it placed at least one of the Hundred-Handers, Aigaion, on the Titans' side. The Greeks never fully agreed on who the good guys were.`,
      `That uncertainty comes through in what happens to the defeated. Atlas is made to hold up the sky at the edge of the world, and Hesiod says he does it by force of necessity, a phrase I can't read without feeling sorry for him. The others are shut inside bronze gates in Tartarus. Yet the Greeks kept returning to them with something like sympathy, and Prometheus, a Titan's son, ended up as one of the most beloved figures in the whole mythology.`,
      `## Why the Giants replaced them in art`,
      `One odd fact about how the Greeks remembered this war: in art, it almost disappeared. When artists wanted to show gods fighting for the cosmos, they usually chose the Gigantomachy, the later battle against the Giants, who were born from the blood of Uranus. The Giants appear on the Parthenon metopes and on the great altar at Pergamon. The Titans, who fought the more important war, are mostly missing from sculpture.`,
      `My own guess is that the Titans were too close to home. The Giants were monsters from outside, easy to hate. The Titans were Zeus's own uncles, aunts and father. Showing them on a temple wall would have meant showing a family quarrel on a building dedicated to the family's winners. It was simpler to commemorate a cleaner enemy.`,
      `## The thing to remember`,
      `So here is what I'd carry away from the Titanomachy. It's the one war in Greek myth that is decided before it begins. A mother lies, a baby is hidden, and a sound is used to cover a sound. The rest, the mountains and the stones and the chains, is the consequence. Cronus lost because he was suspicious of the wrong person and trusting about the wrong thing. If you want to know who really won, it was the people who made noise outside the cave.`,
    ],
  },

  "prometheus-fire": {
    mythId: "prometheus-fire",
    title: "The Relay",
    excerpt: "The fennel stalk is a real detail with a real trick behind it, and the Athenians turned the whole myth into a footrace.",
    body: [
      `Everyone knows that Prometheus stole fire. Fewer people ask how. The answer in Hesiod is a detail so ordinary that it's easy to skip: he carried it in a hollow stalk of giant fennel. That is not a poetic flourish. It's a piece of practical knowledge, and once you know why it works, the whole myth looks different to me. It stops being a story about a theft and starts being a story about how a flame gets passed from one hand to another.`,
      `## Why fennel`,
      `The plant is called Ferula communis, and it grows all over Greece. The stalk is tall and woody on the outside, with a soft pith inside. If you light the pith, it burns very slowly, and the hard outer shell keeps it from going out. People in the ancient Mediterranean used the stalks to carry embers from one place to another, as a kind of portable hearth.`,
      `So when Hesiod says Prometheus stole fire in a hollow fennel stalk, he is describing exactly how a Greek farmer would have moved a flame. The god does the thing a person would do. It makes the whole theft feel less like a heist and more like a favour that someone has passed along, with a practical tool for carrying it.`,
      `I find that quietly moving. The myth could have had him steal lightning or snatch a coal in his bare hands. Instead he does something ordinary, with a plant that every audience member had seen. The great divine crime is performed with kitchen equipment.`,
      `## What Zeus was punishing`,
      `Here's where I want to push on the usual reading. We say Zeus was angry that Prometheus stole fire, but Hesiod tells it a bit differently. Zeus had hidden fire in the first place because of the trick over the sacrifice. He was holding it back as a punishment, and Prometheus took it back. So the theft is the second round of an argument that had already started, and it's the second round in which the human side wins something.`,
      `The reaction is out of proportion, in my view, because the stakes are not really about fire. They are about whether humans can do things without the gods' permission. Fire is the first technology. It lets you cook, forge metal, bake clay, clear land, keep warm. Once people have it, they don't have to ask. Zeus's punishment is aimed at that independence, and it comes in the form of Pandora, who arrives as a gift and turns out to be a bill.`,
      `## The two Prometheuses`,
      `The two main sources disagree about what kind of character he is, and I think the disagreement is the best part. In Hesiod, he is a trickster who gets what he deserves. The poet is hostile. Prometheus is crooked, scheming, and the one who is responsible for the hard life that humans now have. In Aeschylus's play Prometheus Bound, which probably dates from the fifth century BC, he is the opposite: a champion, nailed to a crag at the edge of the world, who refuses to apologise or give up the secret that could save Zeus's throne.`,
      `The play makes him almost insufferably noble, and Zeus, who never appears on stage, comes across as a bully. Between those two portraits you can watch the Greeks changing their mind about what technology means. For Hesiod, a farmer, fire and the toil that came with it were a curse. For Aeschylus, writing in a rich and ambitious Athens, they were a gift worth suffering for.`,
      `## The end of the punishment`,
      `The chained Prometheus doesn't stay chained. In later versions, Heracles passes by, shoots the eagle that eats his liver every day, and frees him. And there's a stranger twist, reported by Apollodorus: Chiron, the centaur, who was wounded and immortal and in terrible pain, agreed to give up his immortality and die in Prometheus's place. One undying being replaced another.`,
      `I find that deeply odd and I think it's meant to be. The mortal world gets fire, a god pays for it, and the bill is eventually settled with the voluntary death of a third party. It's the only way the books can balance. Somebody always pays, but it doesn't have to be the same person.`,
      `## The footrace`,
      `Now the part that I think is the most delightful. In Athens, there was a festival called the Prometheia, in honour of Prometheus and Hephaestus, and a main feature was a torch race. Runners carried lit torches from an altar of Prometheus in the Academy to the city, and the winner was the first runner whose torch was still burning when he arrived. If your torch went out, you lost.`,
      `Think about what that is doing. The myth is about a flame that has to be carried safely from one place to another. The ritual makes you perform it with your own legs. The point is not speed alone, but keeping the fire alive while you run. It's a very physical way of understanding the story, and it explains why the god was remembered by people who made things: potters, smiths, anyone who relied on a fire that couldn't be allowed to go out.`,
      `Pausanias adds that at Panopeus in Phocis, there were large lumps of clay that smelled like human skin, said to be left over from when Prometheus modelled the first people. So even the creation of humanity is attributed to a potter, a man who works with fire and clay.`,
      `## What I take from it`,
      `My own reading is that the myth is about relay. Fire doesn't belong to anyone. It travels from hand to hand, stalk to stalk, torch to torch, and the real crime is to stop it moving. Zeus's mistake is to hide it in the first place. Prometheus's offence, if it is one, is to make sure it keeps going. Every kitchen hearth, every forge and every torch race in Greece was a small piece of evidence that he succeeded.`,
    ],
  },

  "birth-of-athena": {
    mythId: "birth-of-athena",
    title: "The King Who Swallowed His Adviser",
    excerpt: "Cronus swallowed his children. Zeus swallowed a counsellor. The difference explains why Athena has no mother and why she votes the way she does.",
    body: [
      `The story is usually told as a miracle. Zeus gets a terrible headache, someone splits his skull open, and out steps a fully armed goddess. It's a good image and it has been painted a thousand times. But when I slow it down, what stands out to me is something else. The thing Zeus swallowed was not a child. It was Metis, whose name means cunning, practical wisdom, good counsel. He ate his adviser, and the rest of the myth is a story about what happens when a king decides to keep the advice inside him.`,
      `## What Metis was`,
      `Hesiod says Metis was Zeus's first wife, and that she knew more than all the gods and mortal men. She was a daughter of Oceanus and Tethys, so one of the older generation, and when Zeus was fighting the Titans, she gave him the drug that made Cronus bring up his swallowed children, in the version told by Apollodorus. Her help was part of the reason he won.`,
      `Then comes the prophecy. Gaia and Uranus warned Zeus that Metis was going to bear two children. The first would be a daughter, grey-eyed and as fierce as her father. The second would be a son who would be king of gods and men. And there is the old family problem again. A son will overthrow his father, as Cronus overthrew Uranus and Zeus overthrew Cronus.`,
      `Zeus's solution is to trick her. He speaks smoothly, persuades her to shrink to a small size, perhaps a fly in some tellings, and swallows her whole. Hesiod says he did it so that the goddess would advise him about good and evil from inside his belly. He thought that he'd found a way to have Metis's counsel permanently, and to prevent the son.`,
      `## Not Cronus, not quite`,
      `Compare this with what his father did. Cronus swallowed his children, which is a crude way of stopping the future. Zeus swallows a wife and adviser, and the thing he is stopping is a different kind of threat: not a rival, but a source of intelligence that might think for itself. In the most literal way, he is trying to make cunning part of his own body.`,
      `I think that's what makes the myth so revealing about Zeus's whole style of rule. We saw earlier that he rules through allies and distribution. But here he is doing the opposite. He is concentrating good counsel in one place, and that place is himself. A ruler who truly trusts his advisers does not need to eat them. A ruler who swallows his adviser has decided that the voice should stay private.`,
      `## The headache`,
      `The result is a pain so severe that the story is told in different ways. In the common version, Zeus calls for help and Hephaestus, or in other tellings Prometheus or Hermes, strikes his head with an axe. The skull splits open and out comes Athena, armed and shouting a war cry. The Homeric Hymn to Athena says that great Olympus shook, the earth groaned, and the sun god Helios stopped his horses for a long moment until the goddess had taken off her armour. Pindar adds, in his seventh Olympian Ode, that Zeus rained gold on the island of Rhodes.`,
      `Look at what has happened. The pregnancy that Zeus tried to prevent has happened anyway, only in the wrong place. The daughter was born out of his head, and so she belongs to him in a way that she would not have if she had been born from Metis. But she is also full of the thing he tried to digest: cunning, planning, strategy. The counsel he swallowed has come back as a person who doesn't take orders from his belly.`,
      `Nobody mentions what happened to the son. The prophecy was about a second child, and Metis never had it, because she was swallowed first. So the story ends with the threat removed and the daughter in place. Zeus got what he wanted, in a form he didn't quite expect.`,
      `## Why Athena votes the way she does`,
      `There is a consequence of this birth that I find fascinating, and it shows up in Aeschylus's play the Eumenides. Orestes has killed his mother Clytemnestra to avenge his father Agamemnon. The Furies, the old goddesses of vengeance, demand his punishment. The case goes to a jury in Athens, presided over by Athena. The votes tie. Athena casts the deciding vote, and she votes to acquit.`,
      `Her reason, as she announces it, is that no mother gave her birth, and that she is wholeheartedly on the father's side. She says it herself. The oddity of her origin becomes a legal principle. Because she had no mother, she weights the father's claim more heavily. A myth about an awkward pregnancy turns into the basis for a landmark verdict.`,
      `I find it remarkable that a story that began as a joke about a headache is cited in court. It shows how seriously the Greeks took origin stories. They weren't just entertaining. They were evidence.`,
      `## Loyal, not obedient`,
      `And yet Athena is no dutiful child. She is the one who sides with Odysseus when Zeus's brother Poseidon hates him. She is the one who stands beside Perseus and Heracles. She argues, sometimes sharply, with other gods. Zeus loves her, and she is the daughter he allows to carry his own thunderbolt and shield, the aegis. But she works on her own account.`,
      `That is the part I like best. The king who swallowed his adviser got an adviser who won't stay in his stomach. She comes out, and then she walks off and gives counsel to whoever she thinks deserves it. The attempt to keep wisdom inside the throne didn't work. Wisdom got loose, in armour, and joined the people who needed it. If I had to put it in a sentence, I'd say that the myth is a story about how you can't own good judgement. You can only hope to be on the right side of it when it leaves.`,
    ],
  },

  persephone: {
    mythId: "persephone",
    title: "The Goddess Who Laughed at a Joke",
    excerpt: "The Homeric Hymn to Demeter has a second half that most retellings drop. In it a grieving goddess is made to laugh, nearly makes a child immortal, and gets cheated out of it.",
    body: [
      `Most retellings of this myth stop when Persephone goes back below. Hades, the pomegranate, the seasons, the end. But the Homeric Hymn to Demeter, our oldest long account, keeps going. Half of the poem takes place after the girl has been taken, and the middle of it is a story about a goddess disguised as an old woman, a household that takes her in, a joke that makes her laugh, and a baby who nearly became immortal. When I first noticed how much of the poem I'd been skipping, I realised that I had been reading the wrong myth.`,
      `## The one who saw everything`,
      `Start with the abduction, because there's a detail in it that matters later. Persephone, a girl called Kore in the hymn, is picking flowers in a meadow with the daughters of Oceanus when she reaches for a narcissus that Gaia has grown as a lure. The earth opens, and Hades rises in a golden chariot and takes her. Her cry is heard by only two beings: Hecate in her cave, and Helios, the sun, who sees everything.`,
      `Demeter searches for nine days with torches, neither eating nor washing. On the tenth, Hecate comes to say she heard a voice but didn't see the face, and the two go to Helios, who tells her the truth. He says that Zeus gave the girl to Hades, her uncle and a perfectly good husband in his opinion. I love that Helios is so blunt. He doesn't try to soften it. The world's most reliable witness reports that the crime was approved by the girl's own father.`,
      `## The old woman at the well`,
      `After that, Demeter does something I think is wonderfully strange for a goddess. She leaves Olympus and wanders the earth disguised as an old woman. She sits down at a well in Eleusis, a town near Athens, where the daughters of King Celeus come for water. She says she was kidnapped by pirates and escaped, and she asks for work. They bring her home, and the queen, Metaneira, hires her as nurse for her late-born son, Demophon.`,
      `Notice what the goddess of grain is doing. The world is starving because of her grief, and instead of confronting Zeus, she has taken a job as a nanny. There's something very human in it. She can't have her own daughter, so she looks after someone else's child. Grief makes her work.`,
      `## The joke`,
      `Here's the moment I'd put in front of everyone. She sits in Metaneira's hall, veiled, silent, refusing wine and food. Nothing moves her. Then a woman named Iambe, a servant in the house, starts to joke with her. In the hymn it's described as jesting and mocking, and later sources, including Clement of Alexandria, report that the humour was obscene. Whatever Iambe said or did, the goddess smiled and laughed, and then agreed to drink a mixture of barley, water and mint, known as the kykeon.`,
      `That drink became central to the Eleusinian Mysteries. Initiates drank it in imitation of the goddess. And Iambe's joking was remembered in the rites too, in the ribald banter that accompanied the procession. The Greeks believed that the way you pull a person out of grief is not by talking about it. It is by finding a way to make them laugh against their will.`,
      `I find this a sophisticated piece of psychology. A famine that threatens the whole of humanity is, for a moment, held up by a servant telling a dirty joke. The cosmos waits on a punchline.`,
      `## The fire and the scream`,
      `Now the strangest part. Demeter decides to repay the household by making Demophon immortal. She feeds him ambrosia and breathes on him, and at night she buries him in the hearth fire to burn away his mortality. He grows like a god. Then one night Metaneira peeks in and sees her baby in the flames. She screams. The goddess is furious, pulls the child out of the fire and throws him down, and reveals herself. She tells the queen that she would have made him ageless and deathless, but that now it is impossible. He will grow up and die like any other man. But because he lay on her knees, he will have honour forever.`,
      `It's a second failed attempt at immortality, and I don't think it's an accident that it sits so close to the main story. In the first half, a girl loses her place in the upper world. In the second, a boy loses the chance to leave the mortal one. Both are stopped by a decision that someone else makes. And both are compensated, not undone: Persephone gets half a year above, and Demophon gets honour.`,
      `## The consolation prize`,
      `Then Demeter demands a temple and an altar at Eleusis, and teaches the rites to the princes of the town. Meanwhile she keeps the grain locked in the ground, and the human race starts to die. Zeus, realising that sacrifices will stop, sends Hermes down to bring Persephone back. Hades agrees, but before she goes he slips her a pomegranate seed. In the hymn it's just one seed. Later poets increase it to as many as seven, and the length of her stay below grows or shrinks accordingly.`,
      `The hymn ends by promising that the people who have seen the mysteries are blessed, and that those who haven't will have no share in good things when they are dead. That promise, I think, is the real point of the poem. Humans can't be made immortal. But they can be given a ritual that lets them face death with something like hope.`,
      `## What I take from it`,
      `So here's my reading. The famous half of the myth gives us the seasons. The neglected half gives us the Mysteries, and it does so by running the same pattern twice: something is lost, something is offered in exchange, and the exchange is never quite fair. The goddess who laughs at a dirty joke and nearly burns a baby into a god is more complicated than the weeping mother we usually get. She is a person who has tried every route to undo a loss, and finally settles for a ceremony that makes the loss bearable.`,
    ],
  },

  labyrinth: {
    mythId: "labyrinth",
    title: "A Story Held Together by Broken Promises",
    excerpt: "Two promises open and close the Labyrinth myth. Neither one is kept, and everything in between is the bill.",
    body: [
      `The Labyrinth is remembered as a story about a monster in a maze. I think it's actually a story about promises, and the monster and the maze are what happens when people don't keep them. There are two promises in it, one at the start and one at the end, and both are broken by careless men. Everything else, the tribute, the thread, the abandoned princess, the crown in the sky, is the fallout.`,
      `## The bull that wasn't sacrificed`,
      `Start at the beginning, which is not with Theseus. It is with Minos, the king of Crete, who wanted to prove that the gods favoured his claim to the throne. He prayed to Poseidon to send a bull from the sea, promising to sacrifice it. Poseidon did. It was a magnificent animal. And Minos, enchanted by it, put it in his own herd and sacrificed a different one.`,
      `That's the first broken promise, and it's tiny. A swap of one bull for another. But the god notices. In punishment, Poseidon, or in some versions Aphrodite, makes Minos's wife Pasiphae fall in love with the bull. She asks the craftsman Daedalus for help. He builds a wooden cow and she hides inside it. The result is the Minotaur, a man's body with a bull's head, whose name means the bull of Minos.`,
      `Minos is ashamed and has Daedalus build a structure to hold the creature. That is the Labyrinth. The name may come from labrys, a Cretan word for the double axe, and the great palace at Knossos, with its mazelike corridors, has often been suggested as the source of the legend. Whether or not that's right, it's a lovely idea: that a real building became a story because it was too complicated to explain.`,
      `## The debt`,
      `Next comes the tribute. Minos's son Androgeos was killed in Athens, by the Athenians according to most versions, and Minos took revenge. He attacked the city and demanded, every nine years in some tellings, every year in others, seven young men and seven young women to be sent to Crete. They were put into the Labyrinth for the Minotaur to eat.`,
      `Here the whole city of Athens is paying for someone else's mistake, with the lives of its young. I find that striking. The monster is not the villain of the story. It is the instrument of a debt, one that began with a swapped bull and was passed on through two generations. Everyone in the myth is paying for something they didn't personally do.`,
      `## The thread`,
      `Theseus, prince of Athens, volunteers to go as one of the youths. When he arrives in Crete, Ariadne, Minos's daughter, falls in love with him. She gives him a ball of thread, taught by Daedalus, which he ties at the entrance and unwinds as he goes. He kills the Minotaur, follows the thread back out and escapes with the other Athenians and with Ariadne.`,
      `What I like is how the thread works. It's not a weapon. It's a solution to a navigation problem. The Labyrinth was not hard because the Minotaur was strong. It was hard because nobody who went in could find the way back. The thing that defeats it is a ball of string from a woman who understood the building. As a lesson in what counts as heroism, it's wonderfully unglamorous.`,
      `## The abandonment`,
      `On the way home, they stop on the island of Naxos, and Theseus leaves Ariadne there. The reasons vary. In some versions Dionysus appears to him in a dream and claims her. In others he simply forgets, or deserts her. In Homer's Odyssey, Artemis kills her on Dia at Dionysus's word. In the most common later telling, she wakes alone on the shore, and Dionysus finds her, marries her and sets her crown among the stars, where it becomes the constellation Corona Borealis.`,
      `It's a strange moment of moral ambiguity. The woman who made the whole victory possible is left behind, and the myth lets a god step in so that she isn't ruined. But the hero's behaviour is never really excused. The Greeks knew a rotten deal when they saw one.`,
      `## The white sail`,
      `Now the second promise. Before Theseus left Athens, he told his father Aegeus that if he succeeded he would change the ship's black sail for a white one on the way home, so that the old king would know he was alive. He forgot. The ship came into sight with its black sail still flying. Aegeus, watching from the cliff, assumed his son was dead and threw himself into the sea. The sea is called the Aegean after him.`,
      `That is the second broken promise, and it is as small and as terrible as the first. A bull swapped. A sail not changed. One happens at the start, the other at the end, and they bracket the whole myth like bookends. The hero who kills the monster is undone by a lapse of attention that costs him his father.`,
      `## A tidier version`,
      `One of the nicest oddities is what happened when later writers got uncomfortable with the monster. Plutarch, in his life of Theseus, reports an alternative version in which the Minotaur was not a creature at all but a Cretan general named Taurus, whom Theseus defeated in a contest. The Labyrinth, in that account, was simply a prison. The story stays, but the supernatural is edited out.`,
      `I think that tells you how powerful the myth was. People kept trying to make it more reasonable, and kept failing, because the version with the bull-headed man in the maze was the one that stuck.`,
      `## What I take from it`,
      `If I had to say what the Labyrinth is about, I'd say consequences. Every bad thing in it traces back to a small failure to do what was promised, and every attempt to fix it, whether by sacrifice, by cunning or by love, comes with a new debt. The monster is a symptom. The real subject is how a minor betrayal at the top of a family can ripple through two generations and end with a king throwing himself off a cliff. It's a cautionary tale that has nothing to do with strength.`,
    ],
  },

  troy: {
    mythId: "troy",
    title: "A War Made of Obligations",
    excerpt: "Nobody in the Trojan War wanted to be there. They were all bound by something: an oath, a rule of hospitality, a favour owed. The war is what happens when everyone keeps their word.",
    body: [
      `We usually think of the Trojan War as a story about passion, with Paris and Helen as the spark. I'd like to suggest it's really a story about obligation. Look at why each side is there. Hardly anyone in the Greek camp wants to be at Troy, and the reason they have come is a promise they made years earlier, before anyone knew what it would cost. The war does not begin with a kidnapping. It begins with an insurance policy.`,
      `## The wedding nobody could snub`,
      `Start with the apple. The sea goddess Thetis was marrying a mortal, Peleus, and all the gods were invited except Eris, goddess of strife. She turned up anyway and rolled a golden apple among the guests, marked for the fairest. Hera, Athena and Aphrodite each claimed it. Zeus, wise enough not to decide, handed the judgement to a mortal, Paris, a Trojan prince who was herding cattle on Mount Ida.`,
      `This part comes from a lost epic, the Cypria, and I like how petty the cause is. It's an etiquette failure. One guest was left off the list, and the response was to throw a grenade. The war begins because someone felt excluded from a party, which every reader recognises.`,
      `The three goddesses each offered a bribe. Hera offered rule over many lands. Athena offered skill in war. Aphrodite offered the most beautiful woman in the world. Paris chose Aphrodite, and the woman was Helen, who was already married to Menelaus of Sparta. Notice that the judgement itself isn't really where the war starts. It's just the thing that makes a particular man willing to take the risk.`,
      `## The oath`,
      `Here is the part I think matters most, and it happened long before the apple. When Helen was a young woman, her stepfather Tyndareus had a problem. Every important prince in Greece wanted to marry her, and he was afraid that whoever he chose would be attacked by the rest. Odysseus, who was one of the suitors, offered a solution, in exchange for help in winning the hand of Penelope. He told Tyndareus to make all the suitors swear an oath to defend the chosen husband against anyone who might try to take Helen away.`,
      `They all swore. Helen married Menelaus. It was a clever fix, and it worked perfectly, until Paris carried her off to Troy. At that point the oath came due. Agamemnon, Menelaus's brother, called on the suitors, and the whole of Greece was bound to go.`,
      `Think about the irony. The oath was designed to prevent a war. It was supposed to make the princes into a deterrent. Instead, it made the war automatic. And its inventor, Odysseus, was the very man who tried to avoid fighting when it came. When Palamedes arrived at Ithaca to collect him, Odysseus pretended to be mad, ploughing a field and sowing it with salt. Palamedes put the infant Telemachus in front of the plough, and Odysseus turned the blade aside. The trick was exposed. He had to go.`,
      `## The camp`,
      `Once the thousand ships arrive at the Hellespont, the story slows to a crawl. The Greeks sit on the beach for ten years, raiding the surrounding territory and besieging a city they can't take. Homer's Iliad covers only a few weeks in the tenth year, and it begins with a quarrel, not a battle. Agamemnon takes Achilles's prize, a woman named Briseis, and Achilles withdraws from the fighting in a rage.`,
      `The same obligations hold the camp together and almost break it. The kings follow Agamemnon because they've sworn. Achilles follows him because he has a reputation to keep. And when Agamemnon breaks the rules of honour by seizing a prize that belongs to someone else, the whole machine nearly stops. Homer is much more interested in what honour demands, and what it costs when it is violated, than in who wins.`,
      `## The gods on both sides`,
      `The gods are also bound by who they owe. Hera and Athena, who lost the apple contest, hate Troy and back the Greeks. Poseidon, who built the walls of Troy and was cheated of his wages, is on the Greek side too. Aphrodite, who won, supports Paris. Apollo and Ares lean toward Troy. Each god has a reason, and almost none of the reasons are about right and wrong. They are about grudges and debts.`,
      `Zeus, who loves Troy's king Priam but also owes Thetis a favour, is trapped between them. In the Iliad he is shown trying to stay neutral and failing, and at times he simply forbids the others to interfere, a ban which doesn't last. The king of the gods spends the war trying to balance who owes what to whom.`,
      `## The poem that stops early`,
      `Here's a fact that surprises people the first time they read the Iliad. It doesn't end with the fall of Troy. It doesn't even show it. The poem ends with the funeral of Hector, the Trojan hero, after Achilles has killed him and then, moved by Priam's plea, handed back the body. The wooden horse, the sack of the city, the deaths of Priam and Astyanax all happen in other poems, and in later retellings.`,
      `I think that choice is deliberate. Homer stops at the moment when two enemies, bound by their own duties, find a point of shared humanity. Achilles gives Hector's body back because the old man reminds him of his father. For a moment the chain of obligations loosens. And then the poem ends, and everyone knows that it will tighten again.`,
      `## What stays`,
      `So when I think about Troy now, I don't think about a beautiful face. I think about a long chain: a snubbed guest, a bribed judge, a clever oath, a mad king ploughing salt, a stolen prize, a returned body. Almost everyone in the story is doing what they promised, and that's exactly what destroys them. It is a myth about the cost of keeping your word in a world where words are made carelessly.`,
    ],
  },

  odyssey: {
    mythId: "odyssey",
    title: "The Hospitality Test",
    excerpt: "The Odyssey is often read as an adventure. I think it's a long examination, and the subject is how people treat a stranger who turns up at their door.",
    body: [
      `The Odyssey is usually remembered as a string of adventures: a Cyclops, a witch, some Sirens, a whirlpool. But if you stop counting monsters and start counting how Odysseus is welcomed, a different pattern appears. Nearly every episode is a test of how a host behaves when a stranger arrives. The Greeks had a word for the custom, xenia, guest-friendship, and the poem reads to me like a long ranking of who passes and who fails.`,
      `## The rule`,
      `First, what the rule actually was. In the Greek world, a traveller who arrived at your door was under the protection of Zeus himself, who had the title Xenios. You were expected to feed him, wash him, give him a place to sleep, and only then ask who he was. He, in turn, was expected not to abuse the welcome. Gifts were exchanged, and the relationship could be passed on to descendants. For people who travelled by sea and had no hotels, it was a kind of social infrastructure.`,
      `Keep that in mind and the poem starts to look like a catalogue of host behaviour, from the awful to the exemplary.`,
      `## The monsters are bad hosts`,
      `Take the Cyclops Polyphemus. Odysseus and his men walk into his cave and politely wait for the owner. When Polyphemus comes back, Odysseus appeals to Zeus Xenios and asks for the customary gift. The giant answers that the Cyclopes don't care about Zeus, and then eats two of the men. His crime is not just murder. It's a violation of guest law, and his punishment follows. A man who treats strangers as food ends up blind in his own cave.`,
      `The same applies to the Laestrygonians, who destroy the fleet with boulders and spears. To the Lotus-eaters, whose welcome is gentle but fatal, because the guests lose any wish to go home. Even Circe, who turns the crew into pigs, is a kind of inverted host. She feeds them, and the food is the weapon.`,
      `Seen this way, the adventures are not random. They are a gallery of what happens to hospitality when it goes wrong, whether by cruelty or by over-kindness.`,
      `## The best host is a slave`,
      `Now the surprise. When Odysseus finally lands on Ithaca, disguised as a beggar, he doesn't go to the palace. He goes to the hut of Eumaeus, his swineherd. Eumaeus is a slave, poor, with a small hut and a few pigs. He doesn't know that the beggar is his master. He simply does what the rule demands. He feeds him, gives him a cloak, and says that all strangers and beggars come from Zeus.`,
      `I think that scene is one of the great moral moments in Greek literature. The best host in the poem is not a king or a goddess. It's the person with the fewest resources, who gives most of what he has. Eumaeus's pigs are slaughtered for a stranger. In the economy of the poem, he's the richest man in Ithaca.`,
      `## The Phaeacians and the performance`,
      `Compare that with the Phaeacians, who also pass the test, but in a more luxurious way. King Alcinous and Queen Arete take in the shipwrecked Odysseus, feed him, hold games, and eventually send him home with a ship and a pile of treasure. They are the model of generous hospitality, and the poem rewards them with an easy, pleasant stay.`,
      `Yet there's a twist. The Phaeacians are cut off from the world, and their ships take travellers home without asking questions. Poseidon is angry that they helped Odysseus, and in some versions turns their ship to stone and threatens to cover their city with a mountain. Even the ideal hosts are not safe from the gods' politics. Hospitality has a cost, and sometimes it is paid by the host.`,
      `## The suitors`,
      `Now the real crime. In Odysseus's palace, a hundred or so young nobles have been camping out for years, courting his wife Penelope. They eat his cattle, drink his wine, sleep with his maids, and make no attempt to leave. They are guests who never go home and who treat the house as theirs.`,
      `That is the exact opposite of the guest's duty. They abuse the welcome. And when Odysseus turns up as a beggar, they mock him, throw a footstool at him, and refuse to share. The poem treats this as the worst of all possible offences. The suitors are not killed because they wanted to marry Penelope. They are killed because they violated the oldest law of the house.`,
      `Odysseus's revenge is bloody, but the poem insists it is just. The suitors are guests who have become predators, and the rule gives the host the right to defend his home. It's the reverse of what happened in the Cyclops's cave, where the host was the monster.`,
      `## The final hospitality`,
      `One last scene I find lovely. When Odysseus and Penelope finally meet, she tests him, as she has tested every stranger. She tells a servant to move the marital bed out of their chamber. Odysseus, furious, says it can't be done, because he built it himself around a living olive tree, so the bed is rooted in the ground. Only he and she knew that. That is how she knows him.`,
      `It's another version of the guest test, turned around. The stranger has to prove he belongs, and the proof is something only a true member of the household would know. After twenty years the one welcome that matters is the one that comes from the bed.`,
      `## What I take from it`,
      `So I read the Odyssey as a map of manners. It ranks every society Odysseus visits by how it treats a visitor, and it puts the poorest hut above the richest palace. The monsters are bad hosts, the nymphs are tempting hosts, the Phaeacians are generous hosts, and the suitors are the worst hosts of all. The moral of the story is not that a clever man can survive. It's that a decent man can only get home if some people along the way will open a door.`,
    ],
  },

  orpheus: {
    mythId: "orpheus",
    title: "The Man Who Knew the Way Down",
    excerpt: "The famous glance may be a later addition. The older Orpheus is stranger: a musician who learned the road to the dead and sold directions.",
    body: [
      `Everyone knows the ending. Orpheus leads Eurydice out of the underworld, doesn't believe she's behind him, looks back and loses her. It's one of the most famous scenes in Western literature. But if you go back to the older sources, you find that the looking back is not always there. The oldest Orpheus is a different figure, and I think he is more interesting: not a heartbroken lover, but a man who knew the road to the dead and made it into a religion.`,
      `## The earliest version`,
      `Plato mentions the story in the Symposium, written in the fourth century BC. He says that the gods sent Orpheus away from Hades empty-handed, because he was a mere harp player who wasn't brave enough to die for love. They showed him only a phantom of his wife. There is no condition, no glance, no loss at the last second. He simply fails, because he didn't have the nerve.`,
      `Other early writers treat his song as a known power. In Euripides's Alcestis, the grieving Admetus says that if he had the voice of Orpheus, he would go down and win his wife back from the dead. The famous cautionary version, with the ban on looking back, comes most fully from Virgil and Ovid, writing in Latin centuries after Plato.`,
      `I find that thrilling. The detail we all think is the heart of the myth may be a Roman invention, added to explain why a story about the power of song ends in loss. For the Greeks, the question was not about patience. It was about whether any art, however moving, can undo death.`,
      `## The singer`,
      `Whatever the ending, Orpheus is first of all a musician. He is the son of the Muse Calliope, and his father is either the Thracian king Oeagrus or, in some tellings, Apollo. He plays a lyre, and his music affects everything. Animals gather to listen. Trees uproot themselves to follow him. Rivers stop flowing. Even stones shift.`,
      `On the Argo, he was the crew's secret weapon. He set the rowing rhythm with his song, and when the ship passed the island of the Sirens, he played louder than they could sing, and the crew was saved. Here's a hero whose weapon is rhythm, and whose most useful trait is the ability to put a group in time with itself.`,
      `## The descent`,
      `When Eurydice dies, bitten by a snake in some versions while fleeing from a would-be rapist, Orpheus takes his lyre down to the underworld. He goes through the gate at Taenarum, charms Cerberus, calms the Furies, stops the torments of Sisyphus and Tantalus, and approaches Hades and Persephone. His song, as Ovid tells it, makes even the shades weep.`,
      `The thing I notice is how thoroughly he knows the route. He doesn't wander. He walks in as if he had a map. That detail is the key to something that most retellings skip, which is that there was an actual religion built around this idea.`,
      `## The gold tablets`,
      `Archaeologists have found, in graves across the Greek world, thin sheets of gold, folded small, inscribed with instructions for the dead. They date from about the fourth century BC onward, and they were buried with people who followed what we call Orphic or Bacchic rites. A typical tablet tells the soul what to expect. On the left of the house of Hades, it says, you'll find a spring with a white cypress beside it. Do not go near this one. Find the lake of Memory, and say to the guards that you are a child of Earth and starry Sky, that you are parched with thirst, and that you want to drink from the cold water that flows from the lake.`,
      `These are directions. They are a kind of travel guide for the afterlife, and they are credited to the tradition of Orpheus, who knew the way down because he'd been. When I read them, I see the real power of the myth. The man who walked in and walked out again, even once, was the best possible authority on the route.`,
      `That explains why the Orphic movement made him a founder, with poems attributed to him on the origin of the gods and the fate of the soul. His journey was not just a love story. It was an expedition, and the report was valuable.`,
      `## The end`,
      `Orpheus's own death is as dramatic as his life. After losing Eurydice, he refuses the company of women. In the common version, a group of Thracian women, the Maenads, tear him to pieces in a Dionysiac frenzy, in some tellings for ignoring Dionysus, in others for spurning their advances. His head, still singing, floats down the river Hebrus to the island of Lesbos, where it keeps prophesying, and his lyre is set among the stars.`,
      `I like how the story finishes. The man whose voice could stop rivers is silenced by a mob, and the voice survives anyway, in a severed head that keeps singing. It's gruesome, but it makes the point that what he made can't be killed.`,
      `## What I take from it`,
      `So here is my reading. The Orpheus myth is two stories wearing one coat. One is the Latin tragedy about a man who looks back, and it's about the weakness of love. The other, older, one is about a mortal who found the road to the underworld and came back with information. For the first, the lesson is that some things can't be undone. For the second, it is that death has a map, and you can learn something from the person who has seen it. Both stories are about the limits of song, but one says song fails, and the other says song can at least carry directions.`,
    ],
  },

  argonauts: {
    mythId: "argonauts",
    title: "The Hero Who Delegated Everything",
    excerpt: "Jason is the leader of the greatest crew in Greek myth and the weakest man on it. That is the point of him.",
    body: [
      `The Argonauts are the Greek all-star team. Heracles, Orpheus, Castor and Pollux, Atalanta in some versions, the sons of the north wind, Peleus, Telamon, a helmsman who could read the stars. And over them all, as captain, a man named Jason, who isn't as strong as Heracles, isn't as clever as Odysseus, and has no special gift that anyone can name. The longer I read the story, the more I think that is the entire design. Jason's talent is not doing things. It is getting other people to do them.`,
      `## The errand`,
      `Start with why he goes. Pelias, who seized the throne of Iolcus from Jason's family, was told by an oracle to beware a man with one sandal. Jason arrived at the city having lost a sandal while carrying an old woman across a river, who was Hera in disguise. Pelias, alarmed, set him a task he thought would kill him: bring back the golden fleece from Colchis, at the far end of the Black Sea.`,
      `It was a political assassination dressed up as a quest. Pelias sent his rival into the unknown and expected him not to return. Hera, who had a grudge against Pelias for neglecting her cult, took Jason's side. And so the project begins with a cynical king, a vengeful goddess, and a young man who has no real plan.`,
      `## The talking ship`,
      `The Argo was built by Argus with help from Athena, who is said to have put into its prow a beam of oak from the sacred grove of Dodona, the oracle of Zeus. The beam could speak. The ship, in other words, carried an oracle in its bows. Later writers treated it as the first great ship, the original vessel of exploration, the one that opened the sea to human traffic.`,
      `I like the idea of a ship that gives advice. It means the voyage is partly guided by something other than the captain. Jason sails with a counsellor built into the timber.`,
      `## The catalogue`,
      `Apollonius of Rhodes, who wrote the Argonautica in the third century BC, gives a long list of the crew. It's a roll call of heroes, each of whom has a specialty. Orpheus keeps time. Tiphys steers. Lynceus has such sharp eyes that he can see through the earth. Zetes and Calais, the sons of Boreas, can fly. Heracles is the strongest man alive. Polydeuces is the best boxer, and Castor is a horseman.`,
      `This is why I call it a team story. The poem is built around the idea that a hard task needs a range of skills, and that the person in charge is the one who assembles them. Jason's job is not to be the best at everything. It's to know who is.`,
      `And the poem is honest about it. At the start of the voyage, the crew votes for a leader, and they pick Heracles, who refuses and says Jason should do it. The strongest man on the ship turns the job down. It's a quietly modern moment: the person with the most raw power doesn't want the paperwork.`,
      `## Losing the strongest`,
      `Heracles doesn't make it to Colchis. At Mysia, his young companion Hylas goes to fetch water and is pulled into a spring by nymphs. Heracles searches desperately and is left behind. The crew sails without him. It's a startling piece of storytelling, because it takes the most famous hero in Greece off the ship halfway through, and the voyage carries on regardless.`,
      `I find that remarkable. The myth is saying that the quest doesn't depend on any one person. Even the greatest hero can be lost, and the others have to manage. A weak captain with a deep bench is a better bet than a strong one with nobody behind him.`,
      `## The obstacles`,
      `Along the way the crew meets a series of dangers, and nearly every one is solved by a specialist. The blind king Phineus is tormented by the Harpies, and the sons of Boreas drive them away. In return Phineus gives advice on how to pass the Symplegades, the clashing rocks at the entrance to the Black Sea. He tells them to release a dove first. Euphemus does so, and the dove flies through, losing only its tail feathers. The Argo follows, and the rocks, having failed to catch their prey, are fixed in place for good.`,
      `That's the earliest example I know of the use of a scout, a test run before committing the whole ship. It's a very practical solution. Nobody fights the rocks. They are tested, timed and passed.`,
      `## The one who actually wins`,
      `In Colchis, the king Aeetes agrees to give up the fleece if Jason can complete three tasks: yoke fire-breathing bulls, plough a field and sow it with dragon's teeth, and then fight the armed men who grow from the teeth. Jason can't do any of it. Medea, the king's daughter, falls in love with him, thanks to Hera and Aphrodite arranging things behind the scenes, and offers to help.`,
      `She gives him an ointment that makes him immune to fire. She tells him to throw a stone among the earthborn men, so that they turn on each other. She puts the dragon guarding the fleece to sleep with drugs and a spell. In every case, the thing that works is her craft, not his courage. Jason wins by accepting help from the most capable person around.`,
      `This is the point I've been building to. Jason's whole career is a series of delegations. Heracles has strength, Orpheus has music, Medea has magic, the ship has an oracle, and the gods have a grudge to settle. Jason connects them. The poem presents this as a kind of heroism, and I think it is one, just not the sort we usually celebrate.`,
      `## The bill`,
      `The cost comes later, and it falls on the person who did the most. Medea flees with Jason, killing her own brother Apsyrtus to delay her father's pursuit. Years afterwards in Corinth, Jason abandons her to marry a king's daughter, and in Euripides's play she takes a terrible revenge. And Jason himself, in one tradition, ends his life sitting under the old Argo, which collapses and kills him.`,
      `That last detail has stayed with me. The ship that carried him to glory finally crushes him. The leader who gave everything away is left with nothing, and the one who really made it possible is the person he betrayed.`,
      `## What I take from it`,
      `So the story is not about a great man. It's about the structure of a great enterprise, and the way credit flows to the person at the top while the work is done by others. Jason gets the fleece, but the poem makes sure we know who earned it. If I had to put the moral in one sentence, I'd say that the Argonautica is the oldest account of what happens when a leader forgets who actually did the work.`,
    ],
  },
};
